  "analysis_conclusion": "On dev (n=60, 11 abstain-expected / 49 answerable), all three signals show heavy overlap between the two groups -- no signal cleanly separates them on this split (separation_gap is negative for all three: top1_score -6.57, margin -3.32, ratio -0.74). Ratio has by far the smallest overlap magnitude, meaning it compresses the two groups' value ranges closest together relative to each other, and is scale-invariant (unlike top1_score and margin, which depend on the corpus's absolute score range and would need re-calibrating if corpus size or BM25/trigram weighting changed). Margin is intermediate. Top1_score alone is the weakest discriminator, consistent with it ignoring how much better the top-1 result is than the runner-up. Tentative conclusion: ratio is the strongest candidate signal to carry into Task 2's calibration-based threshold proposal, with margin as a reasonable second choice if calibration's own distribution favors it instead -- dev's job here is diagnostic only, per the design doc, and this conclusion is not the final threshold-signal choice until re-checked against calibration."
